# Wick — Screens, menus, and audio

This file defines the screens Wick opens on and plays on, what each shows, how a
player moves between them, and the game's audio. The rules the screens present
are in `specs/world.md`, `specs/progression.md`, and `specs/evolutions.md`;
the actions the menus are driven by are bound in `specs/controls.md`; and the
icons, sprites, and sounds the screens draw and play are the produced files
`specs/assets.md` lists. Every figure and every piece of screen copy below
carries the name this specification gives it.

## Presentation

Wick fixes no palette, no font, and no styling for any screen, and each
screen's layout is yours except where a table below places one element relative
to another. The look of the night is yours, and `specs/overview.md` states what
a player reads at a glance in the world. Two requirements are this file's own.
Every piece of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at
the logical stage size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`), the HUD included,
which is drawn over the live world. On every menu the item at `menuIndex` is
drawn distinctly from the others, so a player always sees which item `confirm`
would accept.

## Screens

`screen` names the screen the game is in.

| Screen | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The front door: the title, the tagline, and the main menu. |
| `howto` | How the game is played, in a player's words. |
| `playing` | The night: the world, the lamplighter, and the HUD. The only screen that ticks. |
| `levelup` | An overlay over the held world, offering a level-up's choices. |
| `chest` | An overlay over the held world, showing what a chest did. |
| `paused` | The world held still under `PAUSED_TEXT`. |
| `fallen` | The run's end when health ran out. |
| `dawn` | The run's end when the clock reached dawn. |

### `title`

The game opens here, and the debug surface's `reset` returns here.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `WICK` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `KEEP THE LIGHT` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `LIGHT THE LAMP`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked one above the next under the title and tagline.
`menuIndex` is `0` on arriving. `up` and `down` move the highlight by one item
and wrap at both ends, `confirm` takes the highlighted item, and `back` does
nothing.

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `LIGHT THE LAMP` | Starts a fresh run, defined below, and sets `screen = playing`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Sets `screen = howto` and `menuIndex = 0`. |

The night may show behind the menu, dimmed or otherwise quieted.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as the rules of a system.
It covers:

- that the lamplighter only moves, and that every tool fires on its own, each
  on its own rhythm;
- that everything in the dark is drawn to the light and hurts on touch;
- that what falls leaves gems, that gems raise the lamp's level, and that each
  level offers a choice of one from three;
- that there are six tool slots and six trinket slots;
- that the big ones drop chests, and that a chest transforms a tool at its top
  level when the lamplighter carries its trinket;
- that the night ends at dawn, `10:00` on the clock, and that reaching it is
  the win;
- the controls, naming the keys `BINDINGS` gives each action: the arrows or
  `WASD` to move, `Enter` or `Space` to confirm, `Escape` to go back, `P` to
  pause, and `M` to mute.

`back` returns to `title` with `menuIndex = 0`.

### `playing`

The live night, and the only screen the simulation ticks on. It shows the
world through the view, the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` rectangle centered on the
lamplighter, with the ground pattern repeating in world space so motion reads,
and every enemy, projectile, zone, gem, and pickup inside the view drawn at its
world position. The lamplighter is drawn at the stage center
`(STAGE_CX, STAGE_CY)` (`640, 360`), facing the way `facing` says.

The HUD is drawn over the world and reads against it:

| Element | What the player sees |
| --- | --- |
| Health | A bar filled from its left edge, its filled width `hp / maxHp` of the bar's width, with both numbers beside it, `hp` rounded up to a whole number. |
| Experience | A bar filled from its left edge, its filled width `xp / xpToNext` of the bar's width, labeled with `LEVEL_LABEL` (`LEVEL`) and the current level, as `LEVEL 4`. |
| Clock | The run clock as `m:ss`, counting up from `0:00` in whole seconds, the seconds always two digits. |
| Kills | The kill count. |
| Weapon slots | `WEAPON_SLOTS` (`6`) slots in slot order, each held weapon as its icon with one pip per level held, the pips in a row outside the square the icon is drawn in and the only marks that row gains over a slot holding nothing, and an empty slot visibly empty, drawing neither icon nor pip. |
| Passive slots | `PASSIVE_SLOTS` (`6`) slots, the same way. |
| Cooldowns | On each weapon's slot, its cooldown state: the slot's picture on the tick the weapon fires differs from its picture on the tick before, and a weapon whose timer is `0` shows nothing left to run, its slot drawing the same picture on every tick the timer stays `0`. A Lantern slot's timer covers the set's life and the gap after it together. |

### `levelup`

An overlay over the world, which stays drawn beneath it exactly as the tick
that opened the overlay left it. It shows `LEVEL_UP_TEXT`
(`THE LAMP BURNS BRIGHTER`) and the offers in `offers`, listed vertically in
that order. Each offer shows three things:

| Part | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Icon | The item's produced icon. |
| Name | The weapon's name from `WEAPON_NAMES`, the passive's from `PASSIVES`, or `LAMP_OIL_NAME` (`Lamp Oil`). |
| Tag | `OFFER_NEW_TEXT` (`NEW`) for an item not yet held, else `LEVEL_LABEL` and the level it would become, as `LEVEL 3`. Lamp oil is never held, so its tag is `NEW`. |

`menuIndex` is `0` on opening. `up` and `down` move the highlight and wrap at
both ends, and `confirm` accepts the highlighted offer, as
`specs/progression.md` states. Then, if another level-up is queued, the next
overlay opens with a fresh set of offers and `menuIndex = 0`; else
`screen = playing` and the simulation resumes on the next tick. `back` and
`pause` do nothing here.

### `chest`

An overlay over the held world, opened as `specs/progression.md` states. It
shows `CHEST_TEXT` (`A CHEST OPENS`) and the result in `chestResult`:

| `kind` | Shows |
| --- | --- |
| `evolve` | The evolved weapon's icon and its name from `WEAPON_NAMES`. |
| `level` | The item's icon and name, and `LEVEL_LABEL` with the level it became. |
| `heal` | That the lamplighter was healed `CHEST_HEAL` (`30`), in words of your own. |

`confirm` closes the overlay, sounding no cue: `screen = playing`, and the
simulation resumes on the next tick. `up`, `down`, `back`, and `pause` do
nothing here.

### `paused`

The world held still, with the HUD, under `PAUSED_TEXT` (`PAUSED`). `pause`
returns to `playing`, and `back` abandons the run and returns to `title` with
`menuIndex = 0`. `up`, `down`, and `confirm` do nothing here.

### `fallen` and `dawn`

The two ends of a run. `fallen` shows `FALLEN_TEXT` (`THE LIGHT WENT OUT`) and
`dawn` shows `DAWN_TEXT` (`DAWN`); each shows the run's figures and the same
menu below them. The world may stay drawn beneath.

| Element | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Time survived | The run clock at the end, as `m:ss`. |
| Level | The level reached. |
| Kills | The kill count. |
| Menu | `END_ITEMS`: `TRY AGAIN`, `TITLE`, in that order. |

`menuIndex` is `0` on arriving. `up` and `down` move the highlight and wrap,
and `confirm` takes the highlighted item: `TRY AGAIN` starts a fresh run and
sets `screen = playing`, and `TITLE` returns to `title` with `menuIndex = 0`.
`back` does what `TITLE` does.

## A fresh run

`LIGHT THE LAMP`, `TRY AGAIN`, and the debug surface's `setScreen("playing")`
each begin a fresh run, and whatever the previous run held is discarded. A
fresh run has the run clock at `0:00`, the lamplighter at the world origin
`(0, 0)` with `hp = BASE_MAX_HP` (`100`) and `facing = "right"`, level `1` with
`xp = 0` and `kills = 0`, Taper at level `1` alone in the first weapon slot and
no passive held, no enemy, projectile, zone, gem, or pickup in the world, no
level-up queued, and the spawn timer at `0`, as `specs/enemies.md` states.

## What advances on each screen

`simTime` accumulates the frame's delta time on every frame, whatever the
screen, and input is read on every screen. The simulation is the exception:

| Screen | What advances |
| --- | --- |
| `title`, `howto` | Nothing. |
| `playing` | The simulation, in whole ticks of `TICK_DT` (`1/60`) consumed from the accumulated delta time, as `specs/overview.md` states. |
| `levelup`, `chest`, `paused` | Nothing. The world beneath holds exactly the tick it was at. |
| `fallen`, `dawn` | Nothing. |

The delta time left unconsumed is discarded on any frame or pose that leaves
`playing`, whether a tick opened an overlay or ended the run, `pause` was
pressed, or the debug surface posed the screen, so the accumulator is `0` on
every screen but `playing` by every route.

## Menu navigation

Menus are keyboard only, through the registered actions in `specs/controls.md`:
`up` and `down` move the highlight, `confirm` accepts the highlighted item, and
`back` returns. `menuIndex` is `0` on entering every screen, and on a screen
with no menu it stays `0`. Every move of a highlight plays `menu-move`, on the
level-up overlay included. `menu-confirm` plays when an item of `TITLE_ITEMS`
or `END_ITEMS` is confirmed; an accepted offer plays `choose` alone, and
closing the chest overlay plays nothing. `pause` is read on `playing` and
`paused` alone, the movement actions are read as held values on `playing`
alone, and `mute` is read on every screen.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write, and what it plays are the
produced files `specs/assets.md` lists. Define and play exactly the fifteen
cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus, each cue bound with `api.audio.load`
to the produced file `specs/assets.md` lists. Define and play exactly the
fifteen cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus, each cue bound in the instance's
`initialize` with `api.audio.load` to the produced file `specs/assets.md`
lists, and played from a tick through `world.audio`. Define and play exactly
the fifteen cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `hit` | `CUES.hit` | An enemy takes damage. At most once per tick. |
| `kill` | `CUES.kill` | An enemy dies. At most once per tick. |
| `gem` | `CUES.gem` | A gem is collected. At most once per tick. |
| `hurt` | `CUES.hurt` | The lamplighter takes damage. At most once per tick. |
| `level-up` | `CUES.levelUp` | A level-up overlay opens. |
| `choose` | `CUES.choose` | An offer is accepted, with no `menu-confirm` beside it. |
| `chest` | `CUES.chest` | A chest overlay opens. |
| `evolve` | `CUES.evolve` | A weapon evolves. |
| `pickup` | `CUES.pickup` | Bread or a draft is collected. |
| `fallen` | `CUES.fallen` | The run ends fallen. |
| `dawn` | `CUES.dawn` | The run ends at dawn. |
| `menu-move` | `CUES.menuMove` | A menu highlight moves. |
| `menu-confirm` | `CUES.menuConfirm` | An item of `TITLE_ITEMS` or `END_ITEMS` is confirmed. |
| `music` | `CUES.music` | Loops from the start of a run to its end. |
| `hum` | `CUES.hum` | Loops while Halo or Corona is held on `playing`. |

The first thirteen are one-shot cues, each a distinct sound so the events are
told apart by ear. Each is played on the tick its event happens, or on the
frame for a menu event, and at most once on that tick: a tick on which twenty
enemies take damage plays `hit` once, and a tick that raises several different
cues plays each of those once. A cue is played by a tick or a frame, never by
a pose of the debug surface.

### The loops

`LOOPING_CUES` holds the two cues that loop until stopped rather than playing
once.

- `music` is looping on every frame exactly when `screen` is `playing`,
  `levelup`, `chest`, or `paused`. It starts on the frame a fresh run starts
  and keeps playing through the overlays and the pause, and it stops on the
  frame the run ends, fallen or at dawn, or `back` on `paused` abandons it.
  `title` and `howto` carry no music.
- `hum` is looping on every frame exactly when `screen` is `playing` and a
  held weapon is `halo` or `corona`. It starts on the frame that first makes
  both true, whether Halo was just acquired or play just resumed from an
  overlay or a pause, and it stops on the frame either stops being true.

Both loops are reconciled from the state on every frame, so a state the debug
surface posed sounds, one frame later, exactly as the same state reached by
play.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A looping cue sounds through a single source set to loop, from the frame that
starts it until the frame that stops it, playing its file end to end with no
gap; a cue is either looping or not, so starting one that is already looping
changes nothing.

Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime. The game binds
the `mute` action to the runtime's mute bit and toggles it from any screen,
then mirrors that bit into `muted` every frame. Muting silences a running loop
without stopping it, and the loop returns in place when unmuted. The game
stays fully playable with sound muted.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`api.audio.loop` starts a cue looping, `api.audio.stop` ends it, and
`api.audio.looping` reports whether it is; a file-backed cue loops its decoded
buffer seamlessly, and a cue is either looping or not. Loops are started and
stopped from `update`, like every other cue.

Muting and the first-gesture unlock belong to the engine. The game binds the
`mute` action to `api.audio.setMuted` and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors `api.audio.muted()` into `muted` every frame. A muted loop keeps
looping silently and returns when unmuted. The game stays fully playable with
sound muted.
{{else}}
`world.audio.loop` starts a cue looping, `world.audio.stop` ends it, and
`world.audio.looping` reports whether it is; a file-backed cue loops its
decoded buffer seamlessly, and a cue is either looping or not. Loops are
started and stopped from the game mode's tick, like every other cue.

Muting and the first-gesture unlock belong to the engine. The game binds the
`mute` action to `world.audio.setMuted` and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors `world.audio.muted()` into `muted` every frame. A muted loop keeps
looping silently and returns when unmuted. The game stays fully playable with
sound muted.
{{/if}}

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Touch and gamepad input. Every screen is driven from the keyboard.
- Persistence of progress or settings between sessions. Each session starts
  fresh.
